{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Elohim came upon Balaam</b> — Because this man did not attain the level of prophecy, therefore this language is used of him, for now the word of HaShem came upon him by way of occurrence (מקרה), for the glory of Israel. And therefore Scripture says of him, <b>\"And Elohim came to Balaam\"</b> (Numbers 22:20), for this language is never said of the prophets except concerning one who did not reach that rank. Similarly, <b>\"And Elohim came to Abimelech\"</b> (Genesis 20:3), <b>\"And Elohim came to Laban\"</b> (Genesis 31:24). And it is possible that its meaning is the coming of the will in a utterance from the Most High to the intelligences that grant healing — which are reconciled — for Balaam too fell into a trance and a deep sleep fell upon him, with uncovered eyes:" "And the sense of <b>and Elohim came upon him and He said to him</b>, is that when it happened to Balaam — like the occurrences that happen to men of the holy spirit in their seclusion — that a spirit passed over his face, the hair of his flesh stood on end, terror called him and trembling, and the multitude of his bones He alarmed, and his splendor was turned upon him to destruction, and he falls on his face to the ground, then he said: <b>\"The seven altars have I arranged\"</b> — and this is in the manner of prayer, as I have explained (Ramban on Numbers 23:1). But HaShem did not listen to him according to his intention, and said to him: <b>\"Thus shall you speak.\"</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The phrase אשר יחלימו is textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally. The clause may be corrupt or elliptical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:05.438421373Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}